<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/14th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of the Brabantine Succession</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1356–1357</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of the Brabantine Succession was fought in 1356–1357 over the inheritance of Duke John III of Brabant, whose daughter Joanna and her husband Wenceslaus succeeded him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-count-of-flanders">Louis of Male</a>, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, claimed part of the duchy, defeated the Brabançons at Scheut in 1356 and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> and Mechelen. The Peace of Ath in 1357 gave him Mechelen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-the-brabantine-succession.jpg" alt="War of the Brabantine Succession" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Locator_Duchy_of_Brabant_(1350).svg: Sir Iain File:Duchy of Brabant and Duchy of Guelders (1350) locator map.svg: Sém... · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:War_of_the_Brabantian_Succession_(1355%E2%80%931357)_locator_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
